// A hash map — **written out, because the signature it had could not be implemented.**
//
// This file was four signatures and a long note about a contradiction it had noticed and not
// resolved. `../README.md`'s count says 23 files here are entirely elided; this was one, and
// `../QUESTIONS.md`'s standing advice is that the way to settle a design is to write the thing.
// Writing four lines of `put` settles it in one:
//
//     void put(this, K key, V value) {
//       i32 h = ???(key);
//
// **There is no `???`.** `create()` taking nothing can only mean the language hashes `K` for you,
// and `../DECISIONS.md` settles that it does not — *"Identity hashing is not free … A type that
// wants to be a hash key carries the field itself."* So the surface this file declared was not a
// simplification of the shipped `create(fn[i32(K)] hash, fn[bool(K, K)] eq)`; it was a signature
// with no body, and nothing noticed for a day because nobody wrote one.
//
// ## What replaces it, and it is neither of the two the note offered
//
// The note framed it as a choice between *restore the two funcrefs*, which puts two arguments at
// every call site that are the same two functions almost every time, and *keep `create()`*, which
// needs a story for where the hash comes from and has none.
//
// There is a third: **make the pair a value.**

import { Vec } from "./vec.wac";
import { Bytes, bytesEq } from "./slice.wac";

/**
 * How to use a `K` as a key: hash it, and compare two.
 *
 * One argument instead of two, and — the part that matters — **a value that can be named, shared and
 * derived.** `Key.bytes()` is written once in `core` and passed by everyone, where two loose
 * funcrefs are written out at each call and can be paired wrongly: nothing stops
 * `Map.create(hashBytes, stringEq)`, and this type makes that unspellable because the pair is
 * constructed together.
 *
 * It is the same move `@/packages/fs`'s `Mount` makes — a struct of funcrefs standing in for the
 * interface the language has no way to declare — and the same one `Ticket` makes. Third instance,
 * and the first in `core`.
 */
export struct Key<K> {
  fn<i32(K)> hash;
  fn<bool(K, K)> eq;

  /** The two everything actually uses. */
  Key<Bytes> bytes() { return Key(hashBytes, bytesEq); }
  Key<string> text() { return Key(hashText, textEq); }

  /**
   * A key derived from another — `Key.by(Key.text(), c => c.name)`.
   *
   * This is what `@/packages/git/src/prompt.wac` could not write. That file wants
   * `Map<Change, i32>`, records that it cannot have one, and works around it with `Map<u8, i32>`
   * keyed on `Change.code()` — *"and that is the character"*, its own comment says, unhappily.
   * With this it is `Map.create(Key.by(Key.text(), c => c.name()))` and the map is keyed on the
   * `Change`.
   */
  Key<T> by<T>(const Key<K> inner, fn<K(T)> project) {
    return Key<T>(
      (T t) => inner.hash(project(t)),
      (T a, T b) => inner.eq(project(a), project(b)),
    );
  }
}

export struct Map<K, V> {
  Key<K> key;
  Vec<Bucket<K, V>> buckets;
  i32 count;

  Map<K, V> create(const Key<K> key) { return Map(key, Vec.create(), 0); }

  /** The value, or nothing. Absence is the type — the same rule as `Vec.pop`. */
  V? get(const this, K key) {
    if (this.buckets.len() == 0) { return null; }
    i32 at = this.key.hash(key) % this.buckets.len();
    for (Entry<K, V> e in this.buckets.get(at)!.entries.items()) {
      if (this.key.eq(e.k, key)) { return e.v; }
    }
    return null;
  }

  void put(this, K key, V value) { … }
  i32 len(const this) { return this.count; }
}

struct Entry<K, V> { K k; V v; }
struct Bucket<K, V> { Vec<Entry<K, V>> entries; }

i32 hashBytes(Bytes b) { … }
i32 hashText(string s) { … }
bool textEq(string a, string b) { … }

// `bytesEq` was declared here and is `./slice.wac`'s now — the fourth caller that wanted byte
// equality and the fourth to write its own. `@/packages/box/src/lines.wac` named the gap and this
// file was one of the witnesses without knowing it.

// ## 1. What writing four lines settled that a day of arguing had not
//
// The note this file used to carry is *right* about the contradiction and offered two ways out,
// both bad. It did not find the third, and the reason is visible now: it was reasoning about the
// **signature** — one argument or two — where the problem is that two loose funcrefs are two values
// that must agree and nothing says so.
//
// `Key<K>` is the fourth *value type where two things must agree* in this directory, after
// `@/packages/raster`'s `Rect`, `@/packages/ssz`'s `Chunk` and `@/packages/ts`'s `blank(from, to)`
// — and it is the first where the two things are **functions**. The mis-pairing it prevents is not
// hypothetical arithmetic: `hashBytes` with `stringEq` type-checks under the shipped signature
// whenever `K` is a type both accept.
//
// ## 2. `Key.by` is the piece nobody asked for and two files needed
//
// A projection — *use this key, on this part of that value* — is four lines and closes two entries.
// `@/packages/git/src/prompt.wac` wanted `Map<Change, i32>` and settled for `Map<u8, i32>` keyed on
// a character. `@/packages/box/src/lib/args.wac` wants `Map<u8, string>` for single-byte flags and
// records that *"`u8` is not a key"*; with `Key.by(Key.bytes(), (u8 c) => oneByte(c))` it is.
//
// Worth noticing how it was found: not by asking *what should `Key` have* but by writing the two
// call sites that already existed and were written as workarounds. **The workarounds named the
// missing operation** — `Change.code()` and *keyed on the character* are both projections, spelled
// by hand, in packages that had no idea they were the same shape.
//
// ## 3. What could not be written
//
// **`put` is still `{ … }`, and honestly so: it needs growth.** A hash map doubles its bucket array
// and rehashes, and `Vec<Bucket<K, V>>` where `Bucket` holds a `Vec` is a `Vec` of `Vec`s — two
// allocations per bucket and a pointer chase per probe. The shipped `core/map.wac` is not copied
// here and this file has not earned an opinion about its layout. What `put` would show that `get`
// does not is whether the resize can be written without a second `Key` — it can, since `Key` is a
// field.
//
// **`Key<Bytes> bytes()` and `Key<string> text()` are statics on a generic struct, and their type
// parameter is fixed.** `Key<K>.bytes()` answers a `Key<Bytes>` whatever `K` is, which reads as a
// mistake and is the only way to write *a constant of one instantiation* when statics live on the
// type. A free function `Key<Bytes> bytesKey()` says it better and loses the namespacing;
// `@/packages/core`'s `Slice.empty()` has the same shape and got away with it because `T` is free
// there.
//
// **Nothing says `hash` and `eq` agree.** `Key(hashBytes, alwaysFalse)` builds a map that never
// finds anything, and `Key(alwaysZero, bytesEq)` builds one that is a linked list. The pairing is
// enforced and the *law* — `eq(a, b)` implies `hash(a) == hash(b)` — is not, which is the
// associativity absence `@/packages/ssh/src/knownhosts.wac` records, fourth instance and the first
// in `core`.
